e.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/faa9f03d/D11/2/code.py:132:    Key insight: Grey cells are not choices between two visible colors; they are explicit occlusion markers. The surrounding repaired fragments reveal which color continues straight across horizontally and which color continues straight across vertically, so the solver can rebuild connectivity first and only then repaint the crossing as grey.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D11/3/code.py:275:    Key insight: Color alone is no longer enough; the true marker is the component with the right local structure, so scattered same-color pixels must be ignored when recovering the bounding box and diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/d35bdbdc/D4/1/code.py:183:    Key insight: The red singleton markers replace the old path-endpoint cue; once the marked sprites are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/581f7754/D6/3/code.py:72:    Key insight: The anchor still selects the translation axis, but the vacated source footprint is no longer erased completely; it survives as a monochrome marker-colored trail.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D7/1/code.py:172:    Key insight: The bottom-left legend is only an input-side key; it tells you how to color each marker family, but it does not belong in the final image.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/581f7754/D14/1/code.py:73:    Key insight: Separate the anchor's two roles — its edge chooses the motion axis, while its relative position to the marker chooses left/right or up/down; the stopping point is always the boundary, not the anchor line.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a251c730/D1/3/code.py:72:    Key insight: The anchor color still identifies the sprite, but now its placement is inferred from border-aligned row/column cues rather than interior markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a251c730/D4/0/code.py:238:    Key insight: The rare anchor color still identifies the correct sprite, but the marker's location now carries a second piece 