/ceshi/code/a251c730/D15/1/code.py:138:    Key insight: Color still selects the sprite type, but marker geometry now encodes a second instruction: how the matched sprite must be transformed before placement.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D4/0/code.py:170:    Key insight: the transformation logic for each marker color is unchanged; the only new challenge is that the instruction strip must be found on the grid edge before the original fill rule can be applied.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D13/1/code.py:314:    Key insight: The legend still determines each marker's fill color, but overlap resolution is now \"first come, first served\" because later entries can only claim untouched background cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/16de56c4/D16/3/code.py:103:    Key insight: the distance from the inner marker back to the shooting edge is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D18/1/code.py:3:# Change: 通过检测哪两个角有 L 形锚点来确定标记对角线方向（TL+BR 得分 vs TR+BL 得分）... → 额外的方向标记像素\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D18/1/code.py:222:    assert on_h_edge ^ on_v_edge, \"direction marker must lie on exactly one bbox edge family\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D18/1/code.py:223:    assert not (rr in (rmin, rmax) and cc in (cmin, cmax)), \"direction marker may not be a corner\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D18/1/code.py:303:    Key insight: The two L corners only define the rectangle; the lone extra marker pixel chooses which diagonal-oriented half of that rectangle should be colored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/16de56c4/D3/1/code.py:72:    Key insight: the two same-colored marker dots do not merely define a beat spacing anymore;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9385bd28/D6/1/code.py:260:    Key insight: The legend still tells you which color each marker should paint with, but the overwrite hierarchy no longer follows legend position; it follows the visible size of the marked rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/16de56c4/D12/0/code.py:138:    Key insight: the old \"period from two markers\" cue is still local, but the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/16de56c4/D12/2/code.py:270:    Key insight: the puzzle is no longer a single bounded fill. The marker pair\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/16de56c4/D13/2/code.py:125:    - One grid edge is the unique launcher: it contains more non-background dots\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/16de56c4/D6/3/code.py:91:    Key insight: the marker pair still sets a modular beat, but the aligned companion is no longer a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/97d7923e/D8/1/code.py:127:    Key insight: The legend is no longer a separate corner cluster; it is a local marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/16de56c4/D7/2/code.py:81:    Key insight: the geometry is still controlled by the repeated marker pair,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D5/0/code.py:392:    Key insight: The first frame identifies the actor color, and the extra color in later frames is the persistent history of previously consumed markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/97d7923e/D10/2/code.py:174:    Key insight: The legend color is only a positional marker. Height still gives the rank,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D5/1/code.py:295:    Key insight: the aligned nearest marker still determines the next move, but nearby off-axis dots disappear as collateral damage when that landing cell is reached.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/1/code.py:171:    Key insight: The move is no longer announced by special marker colors; the solver must infer source and target roles by comparing visible stack heights across lanes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D1/4/code.py:161:    Key insight: The connector geometry, not red/green marker colors, identifies who sends and who receives; counts are conserved exactly as in the original lane-transfer task.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/3/code.py:244:    Key insight: the tip still tells you the current orientation, but orientation no longer restricts which marker can be chosen; the deciding cue is which axis contains the remaining aligned markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:338:    Key insight: The marker color contains multiple small shapes, but only rotated copies of the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D6/0/code.py:341:    Key insight: The triangle tip still determines which marker is consumed next, but the output now requires replaying the whole remaining motion to color the traversed cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D6/2/code.py:140:    Key insight: The red marker means duplication rather than removal: counts are added into the green lane, while the source stacks remain intact.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D11/2/code.py:205:    Key insight: Two red markers now define a subtraction. The preserved bottom-aligned overlap between the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D6/3/code.py:297:    Key insight: the latest snapshot already stores the past trajectory, so solving only requires detecting the current arrow, reading the remaining markers, and extending the trail with the same direction-to-color mapping.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:294:    Key insight: The sprite's current facing chooses which half-line to search, while each consumed marker dictates the next facing, so the arrows act as turn instructions rather than passive dots.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D4/1/code.py:299:    Key insight: the arrow tip does more than show where the next unique marker is;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D11/1/code.py:443:    Key insight: the triangle tip gives the current facing, and the marker trail is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5dbc8537/D2/2/code.py:67:    Key insight: The source pieces are implicit rather than connected — the diagonal marker pair determines the missing rectangle that must be pasted back into the template.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D4/3/code.py:262:    Key insight: the triangle tip reveals the current facing, but the move is determined by counting aligned markers and taking the second one rather than the first.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D21/0/code.py:243:    Key insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D4/2/code.py:270:    Key insight: The arrowhead and the marker chain are both diagonal, so the next move is determined by the nearest dot on the unique active 45-degree ray.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D21/2/code.py:48:#    - A wall that blocks a wrong-direction marker must place one of its cells on\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D21/2/code.py:333:    Key insight: same-color noise is not all equivalent here; singleton components are edible markers,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5dbc8537/D1/4/code.py:80:    Key insight: Rectangle size only tells you which slots a sprite can physically fit; the decisive matching cue is how many black marker pixels appear inside the hole and inside the sprite.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D5/1/code.py:257:    Key insight: The marker panel is now a two-channel instruction: endpoint geometry controls rotation, and the center color controls whether the rotated composite is mirrored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D18/0/code.py:261:    Key insight: The remaining markers are not just ordered by direction; they also form an equally spaced chain, so the first legal jump fixes the stride for the rest of the simulation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D7/2/code.py:288:    Key insight: The markers still determine where the head travels, but only\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D18/2/code.py:310:    Key insight: Geometry alone is no longer enough — marker color tells you which distances are legal, so some aligned dots must be ignored even though they look reachable spatially.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D3/2/code.py:186:    - The occupied silhouette is still one of the original tiny triangle patterns (L-like or V-like), but the singleton apex color identifies both the launcher cell and whether its shot must be diagonal or axis-aligned.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D22/2/code.py:246:    Key insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D16/1/code.py:353:    Key insight: The 3x3 marker no longer selects a canvas edge/corner. It selects\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a32d8b75/D16/2/code.py:273:    Key insight: The border square no longer tells you where to place the object; the true placement cue is the isolated marker pixel hidden in the canvas itself.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D3/3/code.py:3:# Change: 散落的单个彩色像素（不属于任何规则示例的非背景像素）... → 带方向标记的源像素\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/247ef758/D4/2/code.py:126:    Key insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/195c6913/D9/2/code.py:340:    Key insight: The alternating-turn mechanic still controls each route locally, but the global behavior now depends on visible marker order because later markers must react to trails left by earlier ones.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/332f06d7/D15/1/code.py:237:    Key insight: Geometric connectivity is no longer enough; the reachable set is a directed subgraph extracted from visible one-way bridge markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/332f06d7/D16/1/code.py:135:    Key insight: The border marker decides role assignment, while chokepoints narrower than n still\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D14/0/code.py:237:    Key insight: Triggers still generate perpendicular diagonal rays, but crossings are no longer governed by draw order; blue-line / magenta-ray overlaps become explicit red collision markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/195c6913/D13/1/code.py:88:    Key insight: The trajectory logic is unchanged; the new information channel is that the template no longer disappears, it summarizes each marker's final cycle phase.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D6/2/code.py:159:    Read blue arrow sprites as diagonal launchers, then emit perpendicular rays from trigger pixels touched by those diagonals.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D3/0/code.py:16:#   The input still shows blue blobs, a yellow-capped red launcher at the bottom,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D3/0/code.py:59:    - A short bottom red post with a different cap above it marks the initial launcher column.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D3/0/code.py:62:    - Fire a 1-pixel ray upward from the launcher and activate the first blue blob it reaches.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D3/2/code.py:459:    - A yellow cap sitting above a short red bottom segment marks the unique starting launcher.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D12/2/code.py:67:    Key insight: The green port encoding is unchanged; the only new twist is that the initial activation comes from a four-way central source instead of a single upward launcher.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/0/code.py:153:    Key insight: The only changed cue is the start marker geometry: a lone border pixel encodes the initial ray direction, while green port sides still encode every subsequent emission.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/1/code.py:104:    Build a red ray network by activating blue blobs from multiple bottom launchers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/2/code.py:16:# - Concrete form: the old bottom \"red stem + yellow cap\" launcher is replaced by a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/2/code.py:188:    - The only asymmetric two-color sprite is the launcher. Its protruding yellow tip gives